"P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC", "LAB"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/20/2026", "sessionEndDt": "05/16/2026", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "The aim of this course will be to give students an understanding of anatomy using the dog as a model system. Though this course is geared towards veterinary anatomy, it is relevant to human anatomy. It takes a regions-based approach to generating a map of the animal body and will be split into a lecture portion and lab portion where students will study anatomy in small groups using dog cadavers. By the end of the course, students will understand how different body systems function, how they fit together, and will understand the language of anatomy. Anatomy is the first course that students take in their first year of veterinary/medical school and students will learn the basics necessary to succeed in a graduate/professional level anatomy course.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": null, "catalogEnrollmentPriority": "", "catalogLang": null, "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": null, "catalogAttribute": null, "catalogWhenOffered": null, "catalogComments": null, "catalogPrereqCoreq": null, "catalogPrereq": "", "catalogCoreq": "", "catalogFee": null, "catalogSatisfiesReq": null, "catalogPermission": null, "catalogCourseSubfield": null, "catalogOutcomes": ["Diagram anatomical structures within a single body region in the dog and how those structures work together to form body systems demonstrating understanding of inter-relationships within and between anatomical regions.", "Competently dissect and identify anatomical structures on dog cadavers and in prosection material.", "Be able to use anatomical terminology fluently to describe where one structure is relative to another.", "Apply dog anatomical knowledge to explain broad evolutionary similarities and differences between canines and other animals commonly seen in veterinary practice (as well as humans when relevant)."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "catalogName": null, "catalogNextOffered": null, "lastTermsOffered": "2026SP", "catalogCredits": null, "catalogGradeOption": null, "catalogLocation": null, "catalogCrosslisted": null, "catalogCrosslistings": null, "catalogComeets": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2923, "crseId": 368751, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "BIOAP", "catalogNbr": "3300", "titleShort": "Fish Physiology", "titleLong": "Fish Physiology", "crseAttrs": [{"crseAttr": "0012", "crseAttrValue": "OPHLS-AG", "descr": "AG-Other Physcl & Life Sci Req", "descrformal": "AG-Other Physical and Life Sciences Requirement", "attrDescr": "Distribution Requirements", "attrDescrShort": "DistReq"}, {"crseAttr": "0012", "crseAttrValue": "BIO-AS", "descr": "AS-Biological Sciences", "descrformal": "AS-Biological Sciences", "attrDescr": "Distribution Requirements", "attrDescrShort": "DistReq"}, {"crseAttr": "0013", "crseAttrValue": "CU-SBY", "descr": "Sustainability", "descrformal": "Sustainability", "attrDescr": "Exploratory Studies", "attrDescrShort": "ExplStudy"}], "crseAttrValueGroups": [{"attrDescr": "Distribution Requirements", "crseAttrValues": "(OPHLS-AG), (BIO-AS)"}, {"attrDescr": "Exploratory Studies", "crseAttrValues": "(CU-SBY)"}], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 3535, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "11:25AM", "startDt": "01/20/2026", "endDt": "05/05/2026", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "etw36", "firstName": "Eugene", "middleName": "Thome", "lastName": "Won"}], "pattern": "TR", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "Prerequisite: BIOAP 1100, BIOG 1440, BIOG 1445 or equivalent, or permission of instructor. Priority given to: students with interests in fish/aquaculture."}], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/20/2026", "endDt": "05/16/2026", "addConsent": "D", "addConsentDescr": "Department Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [{"crseAttr": "0012", "crseAttrValue": "OPHLS-AG", "descr": "AG-Other Physcl & Life Sci Req", "descrformal": "AG-Other Physical and Life Sciences Requirement", "attrDescr": "Distribution Requirements", "attrDescrShort": "DistReq"}, {"crseAttr": "0013", "crseAttrValue": "CU-SBY", "descr": "Sustainability", "descrformal": "Sustainability", "attrDescr": "Exploratory Studies", "attrDescrShort": "ExplStudy"}], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "C", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRD", "gradingBasisShort": "Graded", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ANSC", "catalogNbr": "3300", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "ANSC", "catalogNbr": "5300", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "BIOAP", "catalogNbr": "5300", "type": "B"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/20/2026", "sessionEndDt": "05/16/2026", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "Fish Physiology is an introduction to the functional biology of fish. Lecture topics will cover cellular and tissue function within key physiological systems that help fish thrive in a wide range of aquatic habitats, from mountain streams to deep-sea vents. These systems will be illustrated using a diverse variety of fish, while broader comparisons between fish and higher vertebrates will also be drawn. Discussion will integrate aquaculture, fisheries, and environmental contexts, including some of the anthropogenic challenges that fish face today and what can be done to mitigate them. Students will perform a gross dissection to identify anatomy in situ and visit an aquaculture facility (there is no separate lab section).", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": null, "catalogEnrollmentPriority": "", "catalogLang": null, "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": null, "catalogAttribute": null, "catalogWhenOffered": null, "catalogComments": null, "catalogPrereqCoreq": null, "catalogPrereq": "one semester of introductory animal physiology (BIOAP 1100, BIOG 1440, BIOG 1445 or equivalent), or permission of instructor.", "catalogCoreq": "", "catalogFee": null, "catalogSatisfiesReq": null, "catalogPermission": null, "catalogCourseSubfield": null, "catalogOutcomes": ["Characterize various physiological systems that fish use to live in an aquatic environment.", "Describe how these systems adjust to environmental changes in the short and long term.", "Explain physiological differences between groups of fishes.", "Explain how physiological systems in fish may be similar to, differ from, or have evolved into those in terrestrial vertebrates.", "Discuss how unsustainable resource management and human perturbations in the environment affect fish at the physiological level."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "catalogName": null, "catalogNextOffered": null, "lastTermsOffered": "2026SP, 2025SP, 2024SP, 2023SP", "catalogCredits": null, "catalogGradeOption": null, "catalogLocation": null, "catalogCrosslisted": null, "catalogCrosslistings": null, "catalogComeets": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2923, "crseId": 355127, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "BIOAP", "catalogNbr": "3410", "titleShort": "Biology of the Mammary Gland", "titleLong": "Biology of the Mammary Gland in Health and Disease", "crseAttrs": [{"crseAttr"